The intent of this PR is to make `grommet-leaflet-core` less biased towards HPE use cases.

The map on the home screen represents a more "HPE" use case.
The map on `/generic-kinds` represents a non-HPE use case.

### Key concepts:
- Introduced concept of `kind` on Pin
- Introduced concept of `kind`, `size`, `icon`, and `label` on Cluster
- Moved any "status" helper functions/variables into `demo-app`. The logic, messaging, etc. shouldn't be baked into `grommet-leaflet-core`. The caller can drive their own logic, then instruct Pin and Cluster what `kind` to render as.
- Introduced `theme` prop on Map. Theme structure details below.
- Introduced `icon` prop on MarkerCluster (similar to Marker). This allows caller to drive specifics of what `kind` of cluster to render as opposed to baking that into MarkerCluster. More details below.
- Fixed issue where `theme` was undefined by wrapping custom icon in `ThemeContext.Provider`. More details below.

#### Areas for further discussion
- [ ] I'd like to discuss how the Cluster theme objects should merge together to build on the default kind/size behavior. What's there is a half exploration and needs refinement
- [ ] Based on office hours discussion, should the border color not be baked into the hpeLeaflet theme?
- [ ] Handling custom context and the somewhat awkward min/max width approach for dimensions

### Exposing `icon` prop on MarkerCluster

In order to remove status/other business logic from `grommet-leaflet-core`, exposing `icon` on MarkerCluster (like we do on Marker) allows the caller to drive the business logic and `grommet-leaflet-core` to just be a presentation component.

See POCMap, where `getClusterStatus` and `getClusterSize` are helper functions that have been moved out of `grommet-leaflet-core` and into `demo-app` since the business logic is very use case specific.

```
<MarkerCluster
      icon={cluster => {
           const kind = getClusterStatus(cluster.getAllChildMarkers());
           const size = getClusterSize(cluster);

           return <Cluster kind={kind} size={size} />;
         }}
>
```

### Map theme prop + theme structure

Map accepts `theme` prop. It then merges what the caller passes with the theme from their Grommet application and the base `grommet-leaflet-core` theme.

`grommet-leaflet-core` theme structure:

```
{
   pin: {
      default: {
         container: BoxProps,
         icon: JSXElement
      },
      [kind]: (any string, similar to button kinds). Theme object would mirror that of default
   },
   cluster: {
      default: {
         container: BoxProps,
         icon: JSXElement
      },
      [kind]: (any string, similar to button kinds). Theme object would mirror that of default,
      size: {
         small: {
            container: BoxProps,
            label: TextProps
         },
        medium: {},
        large: {},
      },
   },
},
```

### ThemeContext.Provider and undefined theme

In order to leave `grommet-leaflet-core` completely theme agnostic, we want to rely on the theme passed onto a Grommet or Map (if that has been specified) element.

Previously, if we didn't wrap each Pin/Cluster in a "Grommet" tag with `theme` prop, the caller's theme was not rendering.

After some investigation my understanding is:
1. When using styled components, once the component is rendered to the client, styled components inserts a "Context.Provider" that has the theme as the context value. (Resource: https://calendar.perfplanet.com/2019/the-unseen-performance-costs-of-css-in-js-in-react-apps/)
2. When we create our leaflet markers, we are leveraging `ReactDOMServer.renderToString` method which resolves custom React components to a native HTML string.
3. At the stage that the React component is being converted to a static HTML string, styled components has not yet inserted its own Context.Provider and therefore the theme is unavailable.
4. To fix this, in the moment when we are calling `ReactDOMServer.renderToString`, we need to wrap the custom element with a ThemeContext.Provider. This works and I removed all the additional "Grommet" tags from Pin, Cluster, Popup, etc.

Adds the `ɵsetEnabledBlockTypes` utility that can be used when writing JIT tests using `defer` blocks. Intended usage:

```ts
import {ɵsetEnabledBlockTypes as setEnabledBlockTypes} from '@angular/compiler/src/jit_compiler_facade';

describe('deferred tests', () => {
  beforeEach(() => setEnabledBlockTypes(['defer']));
  afterEach(() => setEnabledBlockTypes([]));

  it('should work', () => {
    // test goes here
  });
});
```

